Subject: RE: ACME integration: SDK auth, routing & prompt-governance follow-up

Hi Priya/Sam  quick answers ahead of the Nov 3 session and a sample SDK snippet for auth + tool-calling. We'll demo this live but wanted to surface details now.

1) Prompt rollback/versioning: we store prompt artifacts in an immutable bucket with metadata: prompt_id, version, checksum, author, changelog. The runtime resolves the prompt by prompt_id@version. Rollback = update route config to reference previous version; all calls emit prompt_id+version to audit logs. CI: regression tests reference prompt_id@version and run eval harness on merge.

2) Injection mitigations: two layers  (a) runtime sanitization + template parameterization (no concatenation of raw user content into system prompt), and (b) structured tool schema + allowlist enforcement. Example: if a model returns a tool call like: {"tool":"inventory_lookup","args":"DROP TABLE;--"} the SDK validates args against JSON Schema (inventory_lookup.args must be {"sku": string, "qty": integer}) and rejects malformed payloads before execution. We'll show a red-team example where an adversarial prompt attempts to craft tool args with SQL, and how validation blocks it.

3) Embeddings retention: recommended baseline: 90 days for PII-sensitive vectors (encrypted at rest, access audited), 1–3 years for de-identified analytics vectors. We include a TTL index on embedding metadata and a purge job; we can expose retention policy config in the SDK.

Sample SDK snippet (Node.js, simplified):

const Redwood = require('redwood-sdk');
const client = new Redwood.Client({
  auth: process.env.REDWOOD_API_KEY, // hosted dev
  mtlsCertPath: process.env.MTLS_CERT, // optional for Private
  routePolicy: { env: process.env.NODE_ENV }
});

// call structured tool
await client.callTool('inventory_lookup', { sku: 'ACME-1234', qty: 1 });

// attach prompt metadata for audit
await client.generate({ promptId: 'search_v2', promptVersion: '1.2' });

We will expand this into the quickstart and bring a runnable example to the session.

